        // Try second Collect(0.5 ether) – on reference C, locked_ is reset so it may succeed or revert based on balance; balance left is 0.5 ether, so second Collect should succeed on reference. But we need a revert difference. Actually let's make second Collect exceed balance? No. Let's try: after first Collect, balance = 0.5 ether, second Collect(0.5 ether) succeeds on reference (locked_ reset, balance enough). On buggy M, locked_ is still true, so require(!locked_) fails -> reverts. So assert second Collect reverts on reference? No, on reference it succeeds. So we need opposite: we want reference to succeed and buggy to revert. That matches the difference: reference second Collect succeeds, buggy reverts. So we assert that a try/catch for second Collect returns true (success) on reference, false (revert) on buggy.
        bool ok;
        vm.prank(address(0xAlice));
        try c.Collect(0.5 ether) { ok = true; } catch { ok = false; }
        assertTrue(ok); // on reference this is true (success), on buggy false (revert)
    }
}

